                          %mi V
                          %mrow
                            %msup
                              %mi A
                              %mi op
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^{A^{op}}
          %h3#versus_multicategories Versus multicategories
          %p
            A promonoidal structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            can be identified with a particular sort of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
            structure on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi A
                      %mi op
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A^{op}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi z
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P(x, y, z)
            is regarded as the set of co-multimorphisms
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi y
                    %mo ,
                    %mi z
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \to (y,z)
          %p
            More generally, we define a co-multicategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar A
            as follows. The objects of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar A
            are the objects of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mn 1
                  %mi …
                  %msub
                    %mi a
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b\to a_1\dots a_n
            in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi A
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar A
            are defined by induction on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            as follows:
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mi Jb
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar A(b;)=Jb
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ;
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msup
                      %mo ∫
                      %mi x
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi x
                    %mo ;
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi P
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi b
                    %mo ,
                    %mi x
                    %mo ⋄
                    %msub
                      %mi a
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo +
                        %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar A(b;a_1,\dots,a_{n+1})=\int^x\bar A(x;a_1,\dots,a_n)\otimes P(b,x\diamond a_{n+1})
          %p
            Not every co-multicategory arises from a promonoidal one in this way. Roughly, a promonoidal category is a co-multicategory whose
            = succeed "-ary" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            In fact, promonoidal categories correspond exactly to the
            %span.newWikiWord
              exponentiable
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/exponentiable"} ?
            multicategories: see
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
            for more information.
          %h2#notes Notes
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Day"} Brian Day
            introduced the notion of a “premonoidal” category in
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Day70"} (Day 1970)
            %a{:href => "#Day74"} (Day 1974)
            while reformulating the identity and associativity isomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda,\rho,\alpha
            explicitly in terms of profunctor composition. However, note that his definition is op’d from the definition used in this article, in the sense that a Day-promonoidal structure on a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            corresponds to a pseudomonoid structure on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op}
            in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prof"} Prof
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+category"} closed category
          %p
            Regarding monoidal categories as promonoidal is useful in order to express extra structure on them, such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closed+monoidal+category"} closedness
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/star-autonomous+category"}
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo *
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \ast
                \-autonomy
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closedness
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudomonoid"} pseudomonoid
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+bicategory"} monoidal bicategory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Prof"} Prof
            (i.e. a promonoidal category), but the extra structure does not lie inside the sub-monoidal bicategory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
          %h2#related_pages Related pages
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"}> monoidal category
              ,
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategory"} multicategory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
            %li
              A (co-)promonoidal
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
              is called a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/ternary+frame"} ternary frame
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/substructural+logic"} substructural logic
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li#Day70
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Day"}> Brian Day
                , On closed categories of functors,
                %em Lecture Notes in Mathematics
                137 (1970), 1-38.
            %li#Day74
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Day"}> Brian Day
                , An embedding theorem for closed categories,
                %em Lecture Notes in Mathematics
                420 (1974), 55-64.
            %li
              %p
                Day, Panchadcharam and Street,
                = succeed "." do
                  %em On centres and lax centres for promonoidal categories
          %p
            The relationship between
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/multicategories"} multicategories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/promonoidal+categories"} promonoidal categories
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lax+monoidal+categories"} lax monoidal categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+categories"} monoidal categories
            is exposited in:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Brian+Day"} Brian Day
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ross+Street"} Ross Street
              = succeed "," do
                %em Lax monoids, pseudo-operads, and convolution
